  "configuration_id": "config_034",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_003 (multimodal, multiprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, weighted) achieved the best accuracy so far (0.639), and config_008 confirmed weighted fusion > concat under the same setup. The RNA encoder (pca_n2000) is a simple linear projection and may underutilize the rich single-cell transcriptomic signal. Question: Does replacing PCA with the pretrained scgpt encoder (a learned generative model capturing cell-type biology) improve accuracy while keeping all other settings at their best-known values? Why: This is a single-variable change from the current best, directly testing the most impactful unexplored dimension (RNA encoder quality). If scgpt helps, it opens a productive family of configs (scgpt + various image encoders/aggregations) for the remaining 7 experiments; if it doesn't, we confirm PCA is sufficient and can pivot to image encoder or aggregation changes. With only 2 data points, this high-information experiment is the best use of experiment 3 to reduce uncertainty on a core design axis."
}

